@startuml InventoryBuildFromScratch
title Build the GECS inventory example from scratch
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #F7F7FB
  BorderColor #4A5268
  DiamondBackgroundColor #FFF2CC
  DiamondBorderColor #A67C00
  ArrowColor #4A5268
}

start

partition "1. Decide the model" {
  :Write the first playable behavior and acceptance checks;
  note right
    Start small: one hero, one item, pick up,
    select, use, and see a visible result.
    Expand to stacks, dropping, and item variants later.
  end note
  :Choose relationship-based ownership;
  note right
    An inventory item is an Entity with C_Item
    and an OwnedBy relationship to its hero.
    The inventory is a query, not an array or container.
  end note
}

partition "2. Define data and entities" {
  :Create small data-only components;
  note right
    Core: C_Hero, C_Item, C_Quantity,
    C_OwnedBy, C_IsActiveItem,
    C_HasActiveItem, C_CycleItem,
    C_RequestUseItem, C_PickedUp.
    Add C_HitPoints, C_MoveSpeed, C_Locked,
    and C_SpeedBoost only for those features.
  end note
  :Create Hero, Pickup, and Door Entity scenes;
  note right
    Put gameplay state in define_components().
    Keep scene-node references and overlap callbacks
    on the relevant Entity; callbacks add ECS data tags.
  end note
  :Define item templates as C_Item .tres Resources;
  note right
    Give each type a stable type_id and presentation data.
    Reference use/pickup ItemAction Resources from the template.
  end note
}

partition "3. Make inventory data queryable" {
  :Create cached wildcard Relationship patterns;
  :Add ItemQueries for owned, active, and sorted items;
  note right
    Use an instance-targeted OwnedBy query for one hero.
    Sorting by type_id keeps cycling and UI stable.
  end note
  :Create ItemUtils to compose item templates into entities;
  note right
    Share the read-only C_Item template.
    Add a fresh C_Quantity and deep-copy mutable extras.
  end note
}

partition "4. Centralize inventory mutations" {
  :Implement InventoryUtils;
  note right
    Build operations around add/remove item,
    consolidate stacks, find active item,
    and set_active_item.
    Keep active-item tag and relationship changes
    in one place; emit inventory/active-item events.
  end note
  if (Can one item be added, queried, selected, and removed?) then (no)
    :Fix the data/query/helper layer before adding input;
    stop
  else (yes)
  endif
}

partition "5. Define item behavior" {
  :Create ItemAction Resource interface and ActionQueue;
  note right
    dispatch() queues behavior; ActionsSystem drains
    the queue once at a known safe point.
  end note
  :Add one concrete action and prove it end-to-end;
  note right
    Then add variants: health kit heals and consumes;
    key removes C_Locked but is retained;
    boots add timed C_SpeedBoost and are consumed;
    coin has no use action and stacks.
  end note
}

partition "6. Connect input and the game world" {
  :Implement ItemsSystem for use requests and cycling;
  note right
    Use C_RequestUseItem / C_CycleItem as one-shot
    request tags, and queue structural changes with cmd.
  end note
  :Implement PickupSystem for tagged floor pickups;
  note right
    On overlap, Pickup adds C_PickedUp and OwnedBy(hero).
    The system composes/adds the inventory item and
    removes the floor pickup safely at command-buffer flush.
  end note
  :Implement drop as the inverse pickup action;
  :Implement movement and timed-effect systems as needed;
}

partition "7. Keep presentation reactive" {
  :Add observers for inventory and active-item events;
  :Add component observers for health, buffs, and doors;
  note right
    For example, C_HitPoints emits property_changed;
    O_Door reacts to C_Locked removal.
    Gameplay emits events or changes components;
    it does not directly update the quick-bar UI.
  end note
}

partition "8. Assemble and practice" {
  :Wire World, Entity scenes, systems, observers, and UI;
  :Schedule input before gameplay;
  :Put ActionsSystem last in the gameplay group;
  :Run movement from the physics group;
  :Test pickup → stack → cycle → use → drop;
  :Test health, key/door, and timed-boost edge cases;
}

stop
@enduml
